WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.570
A local model file contains model weights, usually stored as tensors.

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:11.856
Tensors are structured arrays of numbers that represent what the model learned during training or fine-tuning.

00:00:11.856 --> 00:00:14.638
Those numbers are not useful in isolation.

00:00:14.638 --> 00:00:25.235
The runtime has to know the model architecture, tensor names, tensor shapes, precision, and layout so it can map the file contents into the correct computation.

00:00:25.235 --> 00:00:29.872
Metadata turns a model file into something a runtime can reason about.

00:00:29.872 --> 00:00:40.734
It can describe architecture information, context length, tokenizer details, quantization type, special tokens, model family, and sometimes prompt formatting hints.

00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:44.907
This is why a model file is more than a single blob of numbers.

00:00:44.907 --> 00:00:51.531
It is a package of data and instructions that helps the runtime load and use the model consistently.

00:00:51.531 --> 00:00:57.293
Tokenizer data is especially important because models do not process text as raw words.

00:00:57.293 --> 00:01:02.459
Text is broken into tokens, and generated tokens are converted back into text.

00:01:02.459 --> 00:01:08.553
The vocabulary, special tokens, and tokenizer behavior need to match what the model expects.

00:01:08.553 --> 00:01:18.886
If the tokenizer is wrong, prompt text may be represented differently than the model was trained to understand, which can lead to weak or unexpected output.

00:01:18.886 --> 00:01:23.655
Runtime compatibility depends on both the file and the inference engine.

00:01:23.655 --> 00:01:31.868
A GGUF file may be valid, but an older runtime might not support a newer architecture, metadata field, or quantization type.

00:01:31.868 --> 00:01:38.160
Likewise, a runtime may support the architecture but require specific settings to perform well.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:44.784
Local inference is a combination of model file, runtime capability, hardware fit, and configuration.
